#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124292 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f23c5c7-d281-54da-b625-412925c80667" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Differential of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "8ebce398-b228-51b0-8506-e0c6124478a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6852" :question/problem "Find the differential for $f(x,y) = x^2 \\sin y.$" :question/worked-solution "We compute the differential using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124293 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "153739f2-df62-5baf-8438-225edfc33ecb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Differential in a Geometrical Context"}] :question/id #uuid "bb3ab0dc-7e26-58f5-a0c2-564c01c77f2e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6854" :question/problem "Given a cone, find the differential $\\textrm d V$ for the volume $V$ of the cone if the radius is $r=6$ and the height is $h = 3.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the cone is $V = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\pi r^2h.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(r,h) & =(\\frac{∂V}{∂r})\\,\\text{d}r+(\\frac{∂V}{∂h})\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\pi rh\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r=6$ and $h=3,$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(6,3) & =\\frac{2}{3}\\pi \\cdot 6\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{1}{3}\\pi \\cdot {6}^{2}\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =12\\pi \\,\\text{d}r+12\\pi \\,\\text{d}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124294 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Change in a Multivariable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "0c4816ed-fba0-5dc7-94ba-646b875d653d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6875" :question/problem "If $f (x, y) = xe^y ,$ use the differential $\\textrm d f$ to estimate the change $\\Delta f$ in the value of $f(x, y)$ from $(5,0)$ to $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x+x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(5,0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(5,0) & ={e}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x+5{e}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+5\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(5 + \\Delta x, 0 + \\Delta y),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\n\\Delta f \\approx 1 \\, \\Delta x + 5 \\, \\Delta y.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(5,0)$ to $(2,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =2-5=-3, \\\\ Δy & =1-0=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\,Δx+5\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈1\\cdot (-3)+5\\cdot (1) \\\\  & ≈2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124295 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "acafc931-a294-5ca7-8e39-cf8be29f08a9" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Value of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "febc72d4-b1b8-52c9-a805-013b30695077" :question/math-academy-id "e-6876" :question/problem "Estimate $\\sin (3) + \\cos (0.2)$ using a differential of the function $f(x,y) = \\sin x + \\cos y$ at the point $\\left( \\pi, 0 \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $\\left( \\pi, 0 \\right),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\,\\text{d}x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-1\\,\\text{d}x+0\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(\\pi + \\Delta x, 0 + \\Delta y),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\n\\Delta f \\approx  -1  \\,  \\Delta x + 0  \\,  \\Delta y\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\sin (3) + \\cos (0.2)= f(3,0.2),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(3,0.2).$\n\nMoving from $(\\pi, 0)$ to $(3,0.2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =3-\\pi ≈-0.141, \\\\ Δy & =0.2-0=0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈-1(-0.141)+0(0.2) \\\\  & ≈0.141.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.141.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,0.2) & ≈f(\\pi ,0)+0.141 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.2) & ≈\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+0.141 \\\\  & ≈0+1+0.141 \\\\  & ≈1.141.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "acafc931-a294-5ca7-8e39-cf8be29f08a9" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Value of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad66c4fa-3e3f-5ca7-87c6-d4664253161c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.521" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338421} {:answer/id #uuid "16cadda8-e28e-5db4-8ef7-e6850c66d716" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.482" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338422} {:answer/id #uuid "e267b74c-b376-55c6-8f03-ac16d0bfa2a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.652" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338423} {:answer/id #uuid "636f7633-040f-53b2-9ce8-f99518ce5881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.359" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338424} {:answer/id #uuid "7b524090-0612-5634-bad0-3ee845f687ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.274" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "636f7633-040f-53b2-9ce8-f99518ce5881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.359" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338424} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f4f1d83-1e8d-5aa6-8ba2-7b3962fc1a49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "240dd5e9-7623-5d41-a562-b45731698fa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-167171" :question/problem "Estimate $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+1}+0.2)$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{x}+y)$ at the point $({\\pi }^{2},0).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{x}+y)\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $({\\pi }^{2},0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x-\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $({\\pi }^{2}+Δx,0+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\,Δx-\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+1}+0.2)=f({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2).$\n\nMoving from $({\\pi }^{2},0)$ to $({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =({\\pi }^{2}+1)-{\\pi }^{2}=1, \\\\ Δy & =0.2-0=0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈(-\\frac{1}{2\\pi })\\cdot (1)-1\\cdot (0.2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }-0.2 \\\\  & ≈-0.359.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ decreases by about $-0.359.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2) & ≈f({\\pi }^{2},0)-0.359 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+1}+0.2) & ≈\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}}+0)-0.359 \\\\  & =0-0.359 \\\\  & =-0.359.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "153739f2-df62-5baf-8438-225edfc33ecb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Differential in a Geometrical Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8067b52c-da04-51be-83e7-1edb3b741faa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{4}dr + \\frac{1}{8}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122837} {:answer/id #uuid "a3cfa7a3-067d-5243-9361-9adabafe2403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}dr + 8dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122838} {:answer/id #uuid "300d0677-dab4-5c46-b293-044cf57f1aa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{4}dr + 4dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122839} {:answer/id #uuid "6bf20e9e-9e91-5e6b-a6ce-009dc2c92f2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "πdr + 4dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122840} {:answer/id #uuid "6cdb5e3d-2da4-548e-92b9-49e537184e4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{8}dr + \\frac{1}{8}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3cfa7a3-067d-5243-9361-9adabafe2403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}dr + 8dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122838} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3bb73f1-2c84-58a3-bb35-e53f79a07f60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9652424-c2ee-5706-a3c2-84b62902c6d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-37295" :question/problem "Given a sector of a circle, find the differential $\\text{d}S$ for the surface area $S$ if the radius is $r=4$ and the central angle is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the sector is $S=\\frac{\\theta }{2}{r}^{2}.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}S(r,\\theta ) & =(\\frac{∂S}{∂r})\\,\\text{d}r+(\\frac{∂S}{∂\\theta })\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =r\\theta \\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r=4$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}S(4,\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{d}r+8\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f23c5c7-d281-54da-b625-412925c80667" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Differential of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f7d7b48-d777-5bde-a527-4096206df7af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{3}-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}x+(2x+3{x}^{2}y)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338428} {:answer/id #uuid "2e210cd7-3f99-5169-88f7-0dbc6f4eb71b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+{x}^{3}-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338429} {:answer/id #uuid "1d6e71fe-a320-54fb-8bf9-a4c2dd064d5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+3{x}^{2}y)\\text{d}x+({x}^{3}-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338430} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c3a8be-af34-5c06-844e-80ebeed48651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\text{d}x+({x}^{3}-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338431} {:answer/id #uuid "c8904ec3-049b-5e77-b477-9b4a5375ee4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+3{x}^{2})\\text{d}x+({x}^{3}-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d6e71fe-a320-54fb-8bf9-a4c2dd064d5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+3{x}^{2}y)\\text{d}x+({x}^{3}-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338430} :answer-field/id #uuid "a57af259-1b0e-5aa1-b2e3-3cb083ce5a75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b322579f-6a5d-5a60-9e95-cf23bfdd0f6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-79739" :question/problem "Find the differential $\\text{d}f$ for the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{x}^{3}y-{y}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "We compute the differential using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(2x+3{x}^{2}y)\\text{d}x+({x}^{3}-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Change in a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd8a7e49-44a5-5eb2-aa5f-e188de230361" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338463} {:answer/id #uuid "b63ddff9-84da-5e06-acd2-bd26bff884c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338464} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec500fb-9d6b-535f-885b-c3e6cea4a712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338465} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9f1a93-50dc-520d-b3f6-034e4c8f70a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338466} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ad2ee9-88ff-5e3c-bf97-74242a23b602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd8a7e49-44a5-5eb2-aa5f-e188de230361" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338463} :answer-field/id #uuid "45ff3702-d8e8-5826-912c-9b84a056c099" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4c7b68c-e2a2-583a-bdbf-0d748565e794" :question/math-academy-id "q-79810" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+\\sqrt{y},$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(0,4)$ to $(-1,5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2xy\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}})\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0,4),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(0,4) & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}x+({0}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0+Δx,4+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{4}\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(0,4)$ to $(-1,5),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =-1-0=-1, \\\\ Δy & =5-4=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{4}\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈0\\cdot (-1)+\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & ≈0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $0.25.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Change in a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc980bd8-76e4-56aa-b098-dda0efd7cf1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338470} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e5382f-cb04-50cd-9a05-357a6a71400b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338471} {:answer/id #uuid "b3cf4b78-1acf-5e7e-acc0-0a4c1a06fad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338472} {:answer/id #uuid "e61cf5ba-8012-5ed1-927e-cbfb7e293016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338473} {:answer/id #uuid "6e069f8d-fcb8-5999-9c7a-542c943da00d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e61cf5ba-8012-5ed1-927e-cbfb7e293016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338473} :answer-field/id #uuid "4195aeda-3179-5193-a7c1-1a1a96af71bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15304540-cd8c-5704-ac94-52aa9f0b8729" :question/math-academy-id "q-79813" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=xy+x{e}^{y},$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(2,0)$ to $(1,-0.5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(y+{e}^{y})\\,\\text{d}x+(x+x{e}^{y})\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(2,0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(2,0) & =(0+{e}^{0})\\,\\text{d}x+(2+2\\cdot {e}^{0})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+4\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(2+Δx,0+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈1\\,Δx+4\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(2,0)$ to $(1,-0.5),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1-2=-1, \\\\ Δy & =-0.5-0=-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\,Δx+4\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈1\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot (-0.5) \\\\  & ≈-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ decreases by approximately $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "acafc931-a294-5ca7-8e39-cf8be29f08a9" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Value of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e57e6c52-c828-5c7c-b563-65377c9f2d87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.920" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338484} {:answer/id #uuid "e218bd7e-ff26-5214-8942-ab669e441e20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.915" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338485} {:answer/id #uuid "daa6e67f-0752-5b9e-aa6c-be0c42951aac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.925" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338486} {:answer/id #uuid "a1eba0d9-7e20-5ca0-8446-59e8494f2862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.930" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338487} {:answer/id #uuid "40365a43-4300-50a3-ab28-5794d774974e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.910" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daa6e67f-0752-5b9e-aa6c-be0c42951aac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.925" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338486} :answer-field/id #uuid "c608a199-01ac-52ae-ba30-2f6c53784491" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7a466ad-861f-5b55-a550-74aad2ec19b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-79840" :question/problem "Estimate $\\sqrt{4.1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.9)$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ at the point $(4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(4,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(4+Δx,1+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈\\frac{1}{4}\\,Δx+1\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{4.1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.9)=f(4.1,0.9),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(4.1,0.9).$\n\nMoving from $(4,1)$ to $(4.1,0.9),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =4.1-4=0.1, \\\\ Δy & =0.9-1=-0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (0.1)+1(-0.1) \\\\  & ≈0.025-0.1 \\\\  & ≈-0.075.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ decreases by about $0.075.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4.1,0.9) & ≈f(4,1)-0.075 \\\\ \\sqrt{4.1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.9) & ≈\\sqrt{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)-0.075 \\\\  & ≈2-0.075 \\\\  & ≈1.925.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "153739f2-df62-5baf-8438-225edfc33ecb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Differential in a Geometrical Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa44f3c6-8769-51eb-805e-e8f137d31288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338498} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f626ac-ae43-552c-8347-f73795ee47b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338499} {:answer/id #uuid "a4ac7499-57a6-5bc2-9378-221f1148e78b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{4}{5}\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338500} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6abe86-fa8f-581b-a002-e257642411a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{d}r+4\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338501} {:answer/id #uuid "2faef51e-33d2-5208-8d1d-de99b9d98016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{3}{10}\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4ac7499-57a6-5bc2-9378-221f1148e78b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{4}{5}\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338500} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f3551fa-725a-54f5-856a-ec21465a741b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74293020-87b8-5b53-8917-b5f49fa422c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-79958" :question/problem "Given a right cone, find the differential $\\text{d}l$ for the slant height $l$ of the right cone if its base radius is $r=3$ and height $h=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The slant height of the right cone is $l=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{h}^{2}}.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}l(r,h) & =(\\frac{∂l}{∂r})\\,\\text{d}r+(\\frac{∂l}{∂h})\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{h}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{h}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{h}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r=3$  and $h=4$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(3,4) & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{4}{5}\\,\\text{d}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
